# overture-protocol — Intent Tree (this.i)
#
# Source of truth for why this protocol is shaped the way it is. Record each consequential
# decision here first, in its own commit, before the text or code it justifies.

Let an untrusted party ask a principal's agent for an action = goal:
  nid: 8xxflcz6
  why: >-
    Overture lets a requester, identified by its AID but not necessarily known to anyone, ask an agent acting for a principal to have that principal take one action. An agent that admits the overture returns a signed receipt, the requester polls for status, and the agent's status report eventually gives the outcome with verifiable results; an agent may refuse an overture it has not admitted at no cost. It is a standalone, transport-independent set of KERI exn messages. We rejected extending IPEX, because IPEX assumes the parties are already connected and covers only credential disclosure. We also rejected adopting DIDComm coprotocols (Aries RFC 0478/0482), because they assume caller and callee share one trust domain and have no decision step, status or abort. Tradeoff accepted: a new set of messages that the KERI ecosystem must review and adopt, rather than a small extension to one it already knows.
  children:
    Overture messages are KERI v2 exn routes = decision:
      nid: 7cw0yhrk
      why: >-
        Every Overture message is a KERI v2 exn whose route sits under /overture/. The KERI spec defines exn as a generic exchange whose route names the transaction type and step, and IPEX already lives this way, so a route is the intended extension point. In keripy a new route is one handler registered with Exchanger.addHandler, and it inherits signing, CESR attachments, sender authentication, evidence hooks, transport neutrality and KRAM replay protection. We rejected a dedicated message family (a new ilk), because it needs a KERI spec change and parser changes in every implementation, which puts adoption on someone else's timeline; its only gain, top-level fields shaped to Overture, the a block already gives. We chose v2 over v1 for the ri recipient field and KRAM's v2 handling. Tradeoff accepted: a bet on v2 exn adoption while parts of the ecosystem still speak v1.
    Ship first as a stand-alone package that plugs into keripy = decision:
      nid: 630f824j
      why: >-
        The reference code is a separate package that registers Overture route handlers into any keripy Exchanger, and the stand-in agent runs on it. Both ends need the code: requesters are often Signify edge clients (signify-ts) or keripy users, and the agent role matches what KERIA hosts. We rejected building it inside keripy or KERIA from the start, because that makes the design wait on upstream review before it has been exercised, and a plug-in needs nobody's fork or approval. Upstreaming to keripy (building and checking messages), KERIA (hosting) and signify-ts (requesters) follows once the design has held up. keripy's Exchanger passes parsed nested substreams only to /multisig, /ipex and /fwd routes, so the package subclasses it to give /overture/ routes theirs too, until keripy lets a handler opt in; the message format does not change (keri-review-panel finding SPC-F2). Tradeoff accepted: for a while, Overture is a dependency adopters add themselves rather than something already in their stack.
    Requesters run KERI and CESR; there is no thin-requester profile = decision:
      nid: 0p27s4g9
      why: >-
        A requester must sign an overture as a v2 exn (KERI version string, Blake3-256 SAID, CESR signatures, v2 nesting), verify the agent's receipts and status reports against the agent's transferable AID, which means resolving its KEL, and often check KERI/ACDC outcome artifacts (R18). So Overture assumes a KERI/CESR stack on both sides. An Ed25519 key of the kind fiki uses can still be the requester's first identity (R23), and RFC 9421 via fiki remains the HTTP binding's transport authentication (R22). We rejected a thin-requester profile that signs with a CESR subset and trusts fiki response signatures for live checks, because the logic a requester must support is too much to carry outside a KERI stack, and verifying the agent without its KEL had no clean answer. We rejected a JSON-only proposal signed with fiki primitives, because it contradicts the exn envelope (7cw0yhrk, 40r080ck) and loses transport-independent non-repudiation. Tradeoff accepted: a party with only fiki cannot be an Overture requester.
    A hop passes an overture on by attaching it, never by forwarding or wrapping = decision:
      nid: 4uvlnnx4
      why: >-
        When a party passes an overture onward (escalation through any number of agent layers, or the double-consent introduction), it sends its own overture, signed by itself with its own nonce and date, and attaches the original signed message unaltered as a v2 nested substream. Its own overture commits to that exact message by its exn SAID, so its own signature covers the original's date, signer and every overture sent with it, not just the original overture's content (keri-review-panel finding CSR-F1). Each hop is the requester of the next, and only each hop's own send is judged for freshness, so a slow layer never makes the overture stale, and the original requester's date survives inside the original. keripy already does exactly this kind of nesting: its v2 /fwd envelope carries a whole signed exn as a nest, keeps the child's signatures, and commits to it in a.evt (tests/app/test_forwarding.py). We rejected forwarding the original as a live send, because the next agent would judge it for freshness against the original date and refuse it after any delay, and could not tell who passed it on. We rejected wrapping it in a message that says nothing on its own behalf, because that needs a second date inside the overture and makes the relay an anonymous carrier. How agents behind the counter talk is still out of scope; this applies when they choose to speak Overture. Tradeoff accepted: every hop keeps track of which overture below it each of its own overtures was triggered by.
    The engine authenticates only the signature on the Overture message = decision:
      nid: 48r9sm0z
      why: >-
        A party is identified by its identifier (an AID), and authenticated by its signature on an Overture message (an overture, poll or abort), verified against that identifier's key state; who controls the identifier may stay unknown. Keys are not identity: a transferable AID outlives its keys. The Overture engine bases every decision (admission, status visibility, honoring an abort, what reaches the principal) on that one signature. A transport signature, such as RFC 9421 over HTTP, authenticates only the transport exchange to the transport layer, which may use it for transport decisions alone: rate limits, blocking abusive callers, and cheap refusal before parsing CESR. Overture neither requires nor forbids the transport signer to be the requester; an agent may require a match as its own policy. We rejected requiring a match, because it would break hops that pass overtures on (4uvlnnx4), relays, and transports with no signature layer (BLE, NFC). We rejected letting the transport signer inform engine decisions, because that would make the transport layer a party to the protocol. Tradeoff accepted: an HTTP-layer allow-list cannot by itself stand in for Overture admission.
    Requesters find and trust an agent through KERI endpoint authorization = decision:
      nid: 0svwkvee
      why: >-
        A requester finds an agent, and learns that it is the principal's, through KERI's existing endpoint authorization reached by OOBI: the principal signs a /end/role/add reply naming the agent's AID in the agent role (withdrawn by /end/role/cut), and the agent signs a /loc/scheme reply giving its URL. This proves only that the agent is an endpoint the principal named, never what the agent may decide or sign; that authority is shown separately, by delegation or GCD chains. A principal may name more than one agent: keripy keys endpoint authorizations by controller, role and endpoint AID, and its endsFor returns every endpoint AID per role, with agent among the defined roles. We rejected an Overture-specific discovery or registration message, because KERI already provides a signed, revocable, BADA-governed one that every KERI stack resolves. Tradeoff accepted: discovery inherits OOBI's bootstrap weaknesses, which KERI's BADA checks already address.
    A requester keeps an interaction at the endpoint where it began = decision:
      nid: 43lr8z7v
      why: >-
        A principal may authorize several agents (0svwkvee), and an overture's identity is scoped only to the requester (7erf49uk). So the protocol requires a requester, once it has sent an overture to an endpoint of the principal, to send every later message about that overture (a resend, poll or abort) to that same endpoint, or to another location published by /loc/scheme for the agent AID that receipted the overture, never to a different agent's endpoint. A requester may therefore change transport (BLE at a counter, then HTTP from home) without changing who holds the overture's state. The protocol does not require the same agent AID to answer throughout the interaction; it requires only that whichever agent AID answers is one the principal has authorized at that endpoint. How the principal's agents divide or share overture state is behind the counter, and the protocol is silent on it; this constraint is what a highly available implementation, for instance several agents load-balanced behind one endpoint with shared state, must meet. We rejected requiring shared state across all of a principal's endpoints, because it dictates the principal's internal architecture, and rejected scoping an overture's identity to requester plus agent AID, because it would tie an interaction to one agent and forbid that kind of failover. Tradeoff accepted: a requester whose endpoint becomes unreachable cannot move an interaction to another endpoint; it begins a new one.
    Endpoint means the location a requester sends to = decision:
      nid: 2mgg0fn7
      why: >-
        In Overture, an endpoint is a location, the URL a principal's agent publishes through a /loc/scheme reply, to which a requester sends messages. Any agent AID the principal has authorized at that location through /end/role/add may answer there, so one endpoint may be served by several agent AIDs. The endpoint is the agent's general KERI ingress, where key events, replies and exn messages of every route arrive (keripy mounts it at /), not an Overture-specific HTTP path; the exn route inside the message is what selects Overture (7cw0yhrk). This is what 43lr8z7v binds an interaction to. We rejected KERI's sense, where an endpoint is identified by a single eid, because under it 'same endpoint' would mean 'same agent AID' and forbid failover between agents behind one location. Tradeoff accepted: Overture's endpoint differs from KERI's eid-keyed endpoint, so the spec must define the term where it first uses it.
    Outcome is what became of an overture = decision:
      nid: 0d2s9emg
      why: >-
        Overture's term is outcome: what became of an overture, whether the principal acted on it, declined it, or it ended by an event such as expiry or an honored abort. The agent's last message about an overture reports its outcome together with any evidence; that message is named with the message set, not here. We rejected disposition, because it implies every end is a decision, and some ends are only events. We rejected using outcome as the name of the message itself, because the requirements already use outcome for the fact the message reports (the first signed report fixes the outcome), and one word must not name both. Tradeoff accepted: until the message set names that message, text that refers to it must say what it is rather than use a short name.
    'The unit is an overture: one plea for one action = decision':
      nid: 4g7vjv8w
      why: >-
        Overture's unit is an overture: a signed request asking a principal to take exactly one action (R4, R9). Identity, admission, receipts, polls, aborts and outcomes all attach to a single overture, through its SAID (7erf49uk). A batch, if v1 has one, only carries several overtures in one message and has no id or lifecycle of its own. We rejected naming the unit proposal, because the protocol's name should say what its unit is. We rejected request, because every Overture message travels inside an HTTP request, so the request was refused would be read two ways. We rejected letting overture name the batch, because then one overture would name many actions and each id would point inside it. Tradeoff accepted: earlier decisions and the requirements must be reworded from request and proposal to overture.
    A batch carries one or more overtures, each with its own lifecycle = decision:
      nid: 8zysn4nv
      why: >-
        The thing that carries one or more overtures in one message is a batch. Overtures in a batch may depend on one another: an overture references its prerequisites by SAID, the links form a dependency graph, and a prerequisite may be in the same batch (R9). Each overture is still admitted, receipted and given its outcome as itself; the batch has no identifier or lifecycle of its own (4g7vjv8w). A batch is never all-or-nothing: when a prerequisite is declined or fails, each overture that depends on it gets its own outcome, and overtures that do not depend on it proceed. A requester that wants overtures bound together expresses that with dependency links; the protocol does not impose it. We rejected all-or-nothing batches, because they would need a batch-level outcome the batch does not have, and because the dependency graph already says what is bound to what. Dependency links express "proceed only if the prerequisite succeeded", not rollback: an overture already acted on is not undone when a later one fails. We rejected package, because it already names our code's home (630f824j) and is common in KERI and keripy text; envelope, because it implies wrapping one thing, the picture 4uvlnnx4 rejected; pouch, because it says nothing to a reader before it is defined; and overture set, because it is two words and makes polls over several overtures read awkwardly. Batch has no technical meaning in KERI, keripy or HTTP, and R9 and R16 already use it. Tradeoff accepted: readers may assume a batch is atomic, so the spec must say where it defines the term what a batch does and does not bind together.
    An overture's body commits by SAID to the attachments it relies on = decision:
      nid: 8erq0hp5
      why: >-
        A requester's signature on an exn covers only the body, never the CESR attachment groups that follow it, so an attachment is under that signature only if the body names it by SAID. The body MUST so commit to each attached overture (4uvlnnx4) and each item of authority evidence (GCD chains, each committed by its leaf credential's SAID alone, since every ACDC edge holds the SAID of the credential it points to, so the leaf commits to every link; the other links and TEL status travel uncommitted and verify against the leaf, keri-review-panel finding SPC-F3) the overture relies on, because swapping either changes what the overture asserts or exposes a credential the requester did not choose to present. The body of a status report MUST likewise commit to each result it carries as an attachment, because the report states that each result is a result of the overture, and an uncommitted result could be swapped by a relay without the report failing to verify. KELs need no commitment, because a KEL verifies itself; that includes the KELs that show a delegation, since a delegate's inception event names its delegator and the delegator's KEL approves it, so no one can substitute a different delegation (keri-review-panel finding SPC-F3); leaving them uncommitted also lets a relay or agent supply fresher key state while an overture waits. Dependency links are body fields, not attachments. An attachment the body does not name carries no claim by the requester. We rejected committing to every attachment, because it freezes key state at signing and adds bytes for no security gain, and rejected committing to none, because it lets anyone on the path change what the overture claims. Tradeoff accepted: two kinds of attachment follow two rules, and each new kind must be placed in one.

# Terms. One term per concept; `i glossary` renders these. A term is defined here, not in the requirements.

    A malformed batch is refused whole = decision:
      nid: 7f9ydc0f
      why: >-
        An agent checks a batch before admitting any of it. If the batch is malformed (a prerequisite the agent does not know, the same overture twice, or an overture whose signature or SAID commitments do not verify), the agent refuses the whole batch and admits none of it. A well-formed batch is still judged overture by overture: a policy refusal or decline of one ends only the overtures that depend on it (8zysn4nv). Malformation means the requester built the batch wrongly, so nothing in it can be trusted to mean what was intended. Whole refusal also keeps refusal cheap and stateless (R12), and lets the requester fix and resend, since nothing was admitted. We rejected admitting the well-formed part, because it could launch actions the requester believed were linked to ones that were dropped. Tradeoff accepted: one bad overture holds up unrelated good ones in the same batch, a cost borne by the requester that built it.
    A message that refers to overtures may refer to several = decision:
      nid: 0u4svfnq
      why: >-
        Every message that refers to overtures by SAID, whether a poll, an abort, or the agent's status report, MAY refer to several overtures at once. Each referenced overture is still answered or acted on separately, and nothing is all-or-nothing: an abort referencing three overtures may find one already done and withdraw the other two, and the reply says so for each. Such a message is not a batch, because a batch carries overtures, and these refer to overtures that already exist; an abort is not an overture, since it asks nothing of the principal. We rejected one message per overture, because a requester with many pending overtures would pay a round trip for each. Tradeoff accepted: each such message needs a per-overture structure in its reply.
    The agent may suggest when to poll next = decision:
      nid: 0jjt97xh
      why: >-
        A receipt and every later status report MAY carry a suggested time for the requester's next poll. It is never required, and the requester need not honor it; its purpose is to spare both sides a poll every few seconds while nothing can happen, as HTTP's Retry-After does. Because the agent computes it from what is behind the counter, it can reveal who decides or when a person is available, so it SHOULD NOT reveal behind-the-counter facts, and agents are advised to give coarse or jittered values. We rejected MUST NOT, because a principal may not care who learns how it decides, and that choice is the principal's. We rejected leaving polling cadence wholly to the requester, because only the agent knows when an answer is plausible. Tradeoff accepted: an agent that follows a principal's wish to be open about its process may disclose it through this field.
    A requester sees one state, pending, and six outcomes = decision:
      nid: 36j5xvln
      why: >-
        From admission until its outcome, an overture's only visible state is pending; an agent may track internally that the principal has agreed and the action is under way, but never shows it, because that would disclose a decision and its timing before anything is finished, and the requester could do nothing different with it. The outcomes are completed, declined, expired (its start-by passed before it was acted on), failed (agreed to but did not succeed), aborted (the requester's abort was honored), and precluded (a prerequisite did not succeed, so it was never acted on). Expired and precluded are separate from declined because they are events, not decisions (0d2s9emg). We rejected prevented for the last, because it suggests someone acted to stop the overture, and blocked, because it suggests the overture could later proceed. Tradeoff accepted: failed reveals after the fact that the principal agreed, kept because it tells the requester a retry may succeed where a decline would not.
    Every Overture message states what its signature means = decision:
      nid: 7m92u5mp
      why: >-
        No Overture signature leaves its meaning to context. Each message states its signer's stance through its signed route, its signed field names, or, where one form serves more than one stance, a signed meaning field from a closed set. Receipts are such a form: admitted (an agent has admitted an overture as a work item and acknowledges notice of its content, R13) or seen (the signer received the item, with no commitment). The stance result says an artifact is a result of an overture: the agent's status report states it for every result it names, by listing them under a signed results key; a result that can sign, such as a newly created AID, may state it about itself, and the action's catalog entry says when that second signature is required. This reverses 08ehqflx, which let a receipt's meaning come from who signed what; as Who Is Signing? argues (https://dhh1128.github.io/papers/who-sign.html), a signature proves processing, not intent, so an outsider must be able to read the intent from what was signed. We rejected bound, because it named a mechanism rather than the claim and applied to only one action. Tradeoff accepted: a meaning field and a closed set of stances to maintain.
    An overture is verified when received, without anchoring = decision:
      nid: 2j74vu2p
      why: >-
        An agent verifies an overture against the requester's key state when it receives it, and the requester need not anchor the overture's signature in its KEL; a later rotation does not undo admission. Accountability for overtures comes from the signed records the parties keep (receipts and status reports, each stating what its signature means, 7m92u5mp), not from KEL anchoring. We rejected requiring anchoring, because a non-transferable AID has no KEL to anchor in, and requesters may use one (R23), and because it would cost every requester an interaction event per overture. Tradeoff accepted: after a rotation forced by compromise, an agent cannot tell whether an overture signed before the rotation came from the requester or from the key's thief; the requester's remedy is to abort its pending overtures under its new key state, which does nothing for an overture already completed.
    Preconditions are advisory, and an unreadable one bars admission = decision:
      nid: 83e2qr0z
      why: >-
        A requester may state in advance, as preconditions, that its overture should not be started unless it can be carried out in a way that meets them. The set is open: version 1 defines start-by and finish-by, and others (a cost ceiling, a bar on public disclosure) are expected. Preconditions are advice, not obligations on the principal, and a precondition that was predicted to be meetable, but that ends unmet, can only be judged a failure by the requester; nothing in the protocol depends on proving when or how well something finished. An agent must not admit an overture carrying a precondition it does not understand, because advice can be set aside deliberately only by an agent that can read it, and this rule cannot be added later to agents already deployed. We rejected letting agents ignore unknown preconditions, because a later precondition such as a bar on public disclosure would then be silently violated by every earlier agent. Tradeoff accepted: a requester that uses a new precondition reaches only agents that understand it, and can send again without it.
    The signer requirement is a precondition, defaulting to representative = decision:
      nid: 5yen7mup
      why: >-
        A requester's statement of which signer it will accept on a result is said in advance, so it is a precondition (83e2qr0z) and advisory like the rest: an agent should decline rather than start when it can see a result would not be signed as required, and the requester checks the signer of any result itself. Its values are principal (the principal's AID, or a delegate created with the Delegate-Is-Delegator trait) and representative (the principal, or a signer proving a chain of representation to it by KERI delegation or GCD credentials linked through issuer edges). An overture without a signer precondition is treated as carrying representative, and the spec states this so the overture's signature carries no meaning left to context (7m92u5mp). We rejected having no default, because a principal could properly have a third party with no chain to it (a bank or notary) sign a result, which is not the principal's act and not what a requester addressing that principal (R3) would expect. We rejected the earlier MUST NOT complete, because a wrongly signed result cannot bind the requester (R4) and the requester checks it anyway. Tradeoff accepted: a requester that wants a third party's signature must say so with a richer precondition, which version 1 does not define.
    Audience is a precondition = decision:
      nid: 35d29vn5
      why: >-
        A requester asking for a result bound to one downstream relying party (referenced by AID) and that party's nonce is saying in advance not to start unless the result can be bound that way, so audience is a precondition (83e2qr0z), advisory like the rest; the requester checks the binding itself. The result does the binding and the overture only carries the inputs. The nonce is the relying party's challenge: Overture carries it unchanged as an opaque value and does not name it u, because u is entropy an author makes up to make its own block's SAID unique, while this value is chosen by another party so that it comes back in the result; relying parties are advised to make it unpredictable. Audience is not the party on whose behalf the requester acts, which the requester's attached authority evidence shows (8erq0hp5). We rejected making audience a field of each catalog entry, because binding to an audience cuts across every action whose result is a signed artifact, as the signer requirement does (5yen7mup). Tradeoff accepted: an action whose result cannot bind an audience can only be declined when audience is set.
    Purpose is the requester's signed claim, not a gate = decision:
      nid: 6yusrcwc
      why: >-
        An overture may carry a purpose, text the requester writes for a person saying why it asks. It is the requester's claim, covered by its signature, and the requester answers for it; no agent checks it as a condition of admission. It is optional, because a requester cannot know whether its overture will reach a person, and an agent may decline one without it as its own policy. A standard description of what the action does comes from the action's catalog entry, not from the requester. How an agent shows an overture to its principal is behind the counter, so the rule that a person approving should see the exact signed overture, never only a summary rendered from it, is advice to implementers rather than a protocol requirement. We rejected making that a protocol requirement, because the protocol does not govern what happens behind the counter (66u9sk4r). Tradeoff accepted: the main defence against a requester using the purpose to mislead a person rests on implementations following advice.
    Catalog entries sit on the GCD act grid = decision:
      nid: 0nztkj2q
      why: >-
        Each action catalog entry is a JSON Schema for an overture's params, SAIDified and verified exactly by the schema rules of ACDC 1.0 (a bare SAID in $id, and the JSON Schema 2020-12 dialect that ACDC 1.0 requires; later ACDC versions may require another), with its title as a label and its description as the text a person can read (6yusrcwc). An overture's action is that $id, so a signed overture commits to exactly which act it requests, and any change to an entry makes a new entry with a new SAID. Anyone may define entries; the spec ships starter entries and a meta-schema they must satisfy. Two annotation keywords in the entry carry the (effect, state-kind) points the action occupies on the act grid that GCD credentials constrain through acts (https://github.com/bakobo/schema/blob/main/gcd/index.md); an action may occupy several points. Each entry also says which of its parameters designate the act's targets, the grid's third coordinate, because GCD treats acts and targets as one constraint and a policy engine cannot check an act without knowing what it lands on. Placing actions on that grid lets a policy engine behind the counter check an overture against a GCD without knowing Overture. New entries need no protocol change or namespace, and an agent refuses before admission an overture for an action it does not know, as it does an unknown precondition (83e2qr0z). We rejected an Overture-specific classification of actions, because GCD already defines one that delegates' authority is written in. We rejected string identifiers, because they leave a signed overture not fixing which act it asks for (keri-review-panel finding GOV-F1) unless entries are frozen and only the spec defines them, which needs a spec release per action or namespace management. We rejected making each entry an ACDC, because an ACDC needs an issuer AID, and authorship can be added later by an ACDC that endorses an entry by SAID. An entry is not called an ACDC schema, because its instances are params, not ACDCs. The grid-points keyword also carries the SAID of the GCD schema whose act-grid vocabulary the entry uses (its $id; ELDiEui48xsASGvDcNdsOwPptW0v4i-oN6-75_I1d60c today), so a later change to the grid makes a new GCD schema SAID and leaves existing entries with the meaning they were written with (keri-review-panel finding GOV-F1). Tradeoff accepted: the catalog depends on the GCD grid's vocabulary, and an entry must be redefined to use a newer grid.
    A result's links are shown by the outcome report and representation evidence = decision:
      nid: 4662pten
      why: >-
        Every result is a verifiable artifact or a reference that resolves to one, and verifying it needs nothing specific to Overture. That a result is a result of the overture is stated by the status report that gives the outcome (7m92u5mp), which commits to each result it carries (8erq0hp5); a result that can sign may state the same about itself, and a catalog entry may require that. That a result such as a new AID represents the principal is shown by the same representation evidence the signer precondition accepts (5yen7mup): KERI delegation or a GCD chain. We rejected a separate requirement that "where the link is the result, the link is signed", and the bound receipt it used, because those two mechanisms already sign both links. Which proof the relationship-AID action requires by default is a question for that action's catalog entry. Tradeoff accepted: a requester that discards the status report keeps the results but loses the signed claim tying them to the overture.
    An overture's exn carries the principal's AID in ri = decision:
      nid: 6f7mlrg6
      why: >-
        The v2 exn that carries overtures puts the principal's AID in ri, so the envelope itself references the principal (R3) and says what R1 says: the overture is addressed to the principal and delivered to an endpoint the principal authorized (0svwkvee). An agent compares ri with its principal's AID and refuses before admission an overture addressed to any other. keripy's Exchanger dispatches on the route and never reads ri; only route handlers do, and Overture's are its own (630f824j). KERIA addresses the AIDs it hosts the same way. We rejected the agent's AID in ri with a principal field in the a block, because it makes two references do one job and ties the overture to one agent, which R3 deliberately does not. Tradeoff accepted: this differs from IPEX, whose handlers expect ri to be the local hab, and a future keripy rule dropping any exn whose ri is not a local hab would break a stand-alone agent (not a KERIA-style host).
    The overture message carries a batch of self-addressed overtures = decision:
      nid: 5xwgkz20
      why: >-
        The exn that carries overtures leaves x, p and q empty, because an overture's own SAID already ties later messages to it (7erf49uk), and its a block holds overtures, a list of one or more (the batch, 8zysn4nv), and optionally waitUntil, the time until which the agent is asked to hold the exchange open (R16). Each overture is an object with its own SAID in d, which stays the same across resends while the send's SAID changes with dt and identifies the overture (7erf49uk). Each carries u, action and params, and optionally prereqs, preconditions (a map, so an unknown precondition is detectable, R5: startBy, finishBy, signer, audience with aid and nonce), purpose, triggeredBy and evidence. triggeredBy holds said, the SAID of the overture that caused this one, and exn, the exn SAID of the message that carried it, which must be attached whole as a nested substream (4uvlnnx4); the agent refuses the overture unless the attached message has exactly that exn SAID and contains an overture with exactly that SAID. exn alone commits to everything in the attached message; said only selects which of its overtures is meant, because a batch may hold several and two implementations must not pick different ones (keri-review-panel finding CSR-F1). triggeredBy expresses the trigger relation for escalation and introduction alike. evidence lists the SAIDs of the authority evidence relied on (8erq0hp5). Overture-specific fields have expressive camelCase names; envelope fields keep keripy's. We rejected passesOn (it can mean "declines"), relays (in DIDComm and here a relay only carries), parent (on an escalation path it reads as a supervising agent) and prior (it collides with exn p and suggests age). Tradeoff accepted: a hop that relays an overture from a batch must attach the whole batch (F-NAK4).
    A receipt message lists receipts, and nextPoll covers only what it references = decision:
      nid: 9z0lpky7
      why: >-
        A receipt message's a block holds receipts, a list of items, each with its own meaning (admitted or seen, R17), since one message may receipt several things. An admitted receipt carries said, the overture's own SAID (7erf49uk), which is also the digest of the admitted content; a seen receipt carries said, the exn SAID of the message seen. The field is said rather than d because d in an object conventionally holds that object's own SAID. The envelope's i is the signer, ri the party receipted to, and dt the signer's time, which on an admitted receipt is the time of admission. A suggested next-poll time (0jjt97xh) is nextPoll, one per message, and its scope is explicit: the still-pending overtures the message references by SAID, and no others, the same scope as R16's waitUntil hint. Only the agent computes it, so it appears only in messages the agent signs (admitted receipts, status reports, answers to aborts) and is omitted when nothing referenced is pending. We rejected a global scope (no poll before T about anything), because the agent must track every pending overture of the requester and the requester learns nothing about which to ask about, and an implicit scope, because the protocol has no grouping beyond what a message references. We rejected a hint per overture, because separate times show which overture waits on something slow, typically a person. Tradeoff accepted: when one poll mixes fast and slow overtures, the hint follows the fast one and the slow ones are polled early.
    Polls and aborts list overture SAIDs, and waiting is until an absolute time = decision:
      nid: 5zj9qmg0
      why: >-
        A poll and an abort are each signed by the requester, with i the requester's AID and ri the principal's AID (as 6f7mlrg6), and are sent to the agent that receipted the overtures (R2). Each a block holds saids, the SAIDs of the overtures concerned, read in the requester's own scope (R14, R16); a poll may add waitUntil. Both are answered by a status report. They stay two messages, not one with a flag, because the route states what the signature means (7m92u5mp), and asking about overtures and withdrawing them are different stances. The hint for holding an exchange open is waitUntil, an absolute time, in the overture message and the poll: the agent holds the exchange open until that time by its own clock, and answers at once if it has passed. We rejected a relative wait in seconds, because its reference point is the moment of sending, which the agent can only guess, and on store-and-forward links the guess can be off by hours; and we rejected deriving the deadline from dt plus seconds, because it leaves the meaning implicit. Absolute times also match startBy, finishBy and nextPoll. Clock agreement is already assumed, since KRAM refuses a send whose dt lies outside the receiver's window. An abort carries no waitUntil, because its answer reports the state at the moment it arrives. Tradeoff accepted: a sender with a skewed clock gets a shorter or longer hold than it meant, within KRAM's window.
    The agent reports states and outcomes in one message, the status report = decision:
      nid: 30urjn80
      why: >-
        The agent's reports about overtures are one message, the status report. It has one item per overture it references, each with a single status: pending, unknown, or one of the six outcomes (36j5xvln). It answers a poll, an abort, and an overture sent with waitUntil, and it is what the agent sends when it delivers on its own initiative (R16). An item that gives an outcome carries the statement that each result it names is a result of the overture (7m92u5mp) and commits to the results it carries (8erq0hp5); an item that says pending carries none. This names the message 0d2s9emg left unnamed. We rejected a separate outcome report, because an outcome is only the last status, and a poll about several overtures can find some pending and some ended, which would split one answer across two messages or need two kinds of reply. Tradeoff accepted: every earlier use of outcome report becomes status report, and one message type carries two signature stances, distinguished per item by its status.
    An overture is identified by its own SAID = decision:
      nid: 7erf49uk
      why: >-
        An overture is identified by its own SAID, the d of the overture object. The SAID covers everything in the overture but not the send's dt, so every resend of the same content carries the same SAID, and different content is by construction a different overture. Each overture carries u, a salty nonce of about 128 bits of entropy that the requester makes fresh for each overture: the KERI spec's u, which makes the SAID of the enclosing message universally unique (as in xip), and what ACDC v1.1 calls unique entropy. Because the overture's SAID travels in receipts, status reports, prereqs and triggeredBy, the entropy also keeps that SAID from revealing a guessable overture's content. A counter would make overtures unique but not blind them, so u is never a counter. With u, so two identical asks, such as two hamburgers, are two overtures, and an identical ask made a year later is not matched to last year's. Identity is scoped to the requester's AID: an overture with the same SAID sent by another requester is that requester's own. Prerequisite links, polls, aborts, receipts and status reports reference an overture by its SAID. A prerequisite's SAID must exist before an overture that depends on it can be hashed, so a dependency cycle cannot be built. This replaces 69tz0ngx, which identified an overture by a requester-chosen overture id, and 8nyrml77, which made the first version admitted under an id authoritative; together they allowed different content under one id and needed a rule for it, and no use case needed that. We rejected an optional nonce, because a requester that omitted it would have a later identical ask matched to an earlier, possibly completed, overture. Tradeoff accepted: references are 44-character SAIDs where an id might have been a dozen characters, and a requester that changes its mind sends a new overture and aborts the old one.
    The status report lists one status per overture = decision:
      nid: 8ryggr28
      why: >-
        The status report (30urjn80) is signed by the agent, with i the agent's AID and ri the requester's. Its a block holds statuses, one item per overture it references, each with said (the overture's SAID, 7erf49uk) and status (pending, unknown, or one of the six outcomes, 36j5xvln). An item that gives an outcome may carry results, each either {said} for an artifact, the body thereby committing to it when attached (8erq0hp5), optionally with registry for a credential checked by reference, or {aid, oobi} for a new identifier (R18); an aborted item's results list anything already done. reason is optional free text, given only when the principal chooses (R15). nextPoll follows 9z0lpky7 and covers only the items still pending. We rejected also carrying a requester-scoped id beside said, because SAID identity leaves one reference doing one job. Tradeoff accepted: every item carries a 44-character SAID.
    Routes use the established term for each message = decision:
      nid: 970lu92k
      why: >-
        The five routes are /overture/batch (the requester asks for what its overtures say), /overture/receipt (the signer receipts, with each item's meaning), /overture/poll (the requester asks about overtures), /overture/abort (the requester withdraws overtures) and /overture/status (the agent reports states and outcomes). Each route states what the message's signature means (7m92u5mp), under the /overture/ prefix (7cw0yhrk), and each uses a term already defined rather than a new one. The message that carries overtures is a batch by definition (54m97zvg), even when it carries one. We rejected /overture/overture, because it says nothing about what the message holds. Tradeoff accepted: a reader who assumes a batch holds several overtures misreads the common case of one, which the batch term's "one or more" answers.
    Overture is versioned by a.version, and identified by its signed route = decision:
      nid: 3vpll4pd
      why: >-
        Every Overture message carries a required a.version, written major.minor and starting at 1.0. An agent refuses before admission a message whose major version it does not support. A minor version may add only optional fields, which older readers ignore; anything that changes what an existing message asks or means goes where unknowns are already refused, as a precondition (83e2qr0z) or a catalog action (0nztkj2q), or takes a new major version. A message is an Overture message because its exn route begins /overture/; the route is a body field covered by the SAID and the sender's signature, so it is the sender's signed claim (48r9sm0z), as IPEX's /ipex/ routes are, and the handler then checks the a block against that route's schema for the stated version. We rejected the exn's v, because it is KERI's version string and keripy accepts only KERI or ACDC as its protocol, and a field labelled v inside a, because KERI's v always holds a full version string in a fixed format. We rejected a version segment in the route, because routes name the message, not its revision (970lu92k). Tradeoff accepted: route prefixes are not registered in KERI, so another protocol choosing /overture/ would be caught only by the schema check, a weakness IPEX shares.
    Overture signatures are ephemeral evidence; lasting evidence comes from results = decision:
      nid: 7sx6spf2
      why: >-
        Every Overture signature, the requester's and the agent's alike, is ephemeral evidence: it shows what its signer said, judged against the signer's key state at the time, and is adequate because an interaction runs over a short time. Nothing in Overture is anchored (2j74vu2p), so after a rotation no one can prove that a signature was not made with a stolen key in a retrograde attack, and non-repudiation holds only that far. Evidence that must last comes from the results an overture leads to, each anchored by its own mechanism: an issued credential in its TEL, a created AID through its KEL and the uses it is soon put to. A claim that must outlive a rotation, such as notice that shifts liability for a lost license, belongs in such a result, not in a receipt. This answers keri-review-panel finding SEC-F3, which showed that a leaked agent key could backdate an admitted receipt. We rejected anchoring receipts and status reports in the agent's KEL, because it costs an event and witness round trips per receipt and publishes how many overtures an agent admits and when, which reveals what lies behind the counter. We also dropped the argument that an admitted receipt's dt is a claim against the agent's own interest, because a forged admission helps whoever forges it. Tradeoff accepted: a party that keeps only Overture messages cannot prove, after a rotation, what was said before it.
    Overture states its own currency and freshness checks = decision:
      nid: 35wllumn
      why: >-
        Every Overture engine verifies each message it receives against the signer's latest establishment event, and refuses a message whose dt falls outside its freshness window by its own clock or that replays one already accepted. These are Overture's own requirements, not properties inherited from the host: KRAM is one way to meet them. keripy performs both only in KRAM, which Kevery leaves off unless enableKram is set, and without it the Exchanger verifies against whichever establishment event a signature cites and never checks freshness, so a thief holding a rotated-out key could have a fresh batch admitted under the requester's AID, and captured messages could be replayed (keri-review-panel finding SEC-F1). An engine without the checks is not conformant, so the protocol defines no override. The reference package (630f824j) refuses to register its handlers unless KRAM or its own checks are active, and accepts an explicit operator override, for testing, that logs a warning at every start, so no operator loses the guarantees by accident. We rejected leaving the checks to the host, because a plug-in cannot know what its host enforces. Tradeoff accepted: the window's width and the replay rule's exact form are still to be set.
    An attached original's signature is judged against its signer's current key state = decision:
      nid: 3j0mdxh4
      why: >-
        When a hop passes an overture on (4uvlnnx4), the receiving agent verifies the signatures on the attached original message, at every depth of nesting, against each signer's latest establishment event, and refuses the hop's overture if the original's signer has rotated since signing. Only the hop's own send is judged for freshness (35wllumn); the original is not, because it may be minutes or days old. keripy gives no help here: its /fwd handler checks only a child's digest, never its signatures, which is safe there only because the child is later delivered live and checked then, and Overture's original never is. We rejected verifying against the establishment event the signature cites, because nothing is anchored (2j74vu2p), so a thief holding a rotated-out key could fabricate an original and have it believed, the retrograde attack (keri-review-panel finding SEC-F2). Tradeoff accepted: an honest requester who rotates while its overture is being passed on has the hop's overture refused, and the hop must ask it to send again.
    Passing an overture on discloses the whole message it arrived in = decision:
      nid: 7gcqnese
      why: >-
        A requester's signature covers the whole exn body, so a hop cannot cut one overture out of a batch without breaking that signature, and passing an overture on (4uvlnnx4) discloses to the next agent every overture in the message it arrived in, with their params, purposes and evidence SAIDs. The protocol keeps this and says so, and advises a requester to send any overture it expects to be passed on, such as an introduction, in its own exn. We rejected giving each overture its own detachable signature, because a detached overture has no date of its own, which undoes binding the exact send (5xwgkz20, keri-review-panel finding CSR-F1), and because it adds a second signing scheme beside the exn's. This answers keri-review-panel finding PRV-F1. Tradeoff accepted: a requester that batches carelessly discloses more than it meant to, and only advice protects it.
    An overture's SAID follows CESR's SAID protocol over its JSON serialization = decision:
      nid: 48tfl3xj
      why: >-
        An overture's d is a SAID computed by the derivation protocol in the CESR 1.0 specification's section "Self-addressing identifier (SAID)", with the overture object as the self-addressed data, its fields in the order received, and the overture serialized as JSON whatever serialization kind the carrying exn uses. An agent verifies every overture's d by that section's verification protocol, overtures inside attached messages included, and a mismatch makes the batch malformed, so the agent refuses the whole batch before admission (7f9ydc0f). Without that check, one SAID could be reused for different content and taken as a resend. Overture adds only the choice of kind, because that protocol, and the KERI specification's section on SAID fields, leave the kind to the serializer and say it changes the SAID. Pinning it keeps one SAID per overture, so a resend in a CBOR or CESR-native exn is still recognized as the same overture (7erf49uk). An overture has no nested SAIDed blocks, so ACDC's most-compact-form rule adds nothing. keripy's v2 exn computes only the top-level d (serdering.py:469-471), so the reference package computes the nested one itself. We rejected restating the algorithm, because the CESR specification already defines it, and rejected letting the kind follow the exn, because two sends of one overture could then get two SAIDs and run its action twice (keri-review-panel finding SPC-F1). Tradeoff accepted: a requester using a non-JSON exn serializes each overture twice, once for its SAID and once for the carrier.
    A requester never resends an overture it holds an admitted receipt for = decision:
      nid: 5c5fzw97
      why: >-
        Once a requester holds an admitted receipt for an overture, it MUST NOT send that overture again. An answer of unknown after admission means the agent has lost the overture; a requester that still wants the act sends a new overture, with a new u and so a new SAID, knowing the first may already have been carried out. Before any receipt arrives, resending the same overture stays allowed, because that is how a lost receipt is recovered, and the agent recognizes it by its SAID (7erf49uk). Each resend carries a fresh dt, so replay checks pass it, and an agent that had forgotten the overture would admit it as new; for an action that is not idempotent, such as issuing a credential, that runs it twice (keri-review-panel findings SPC-F4 and SEC-F5). We rejected inviting a resend after unknown, which is what made that possible. Tradeoff accepted: a requester whose agent loses state must decide for itself whether retrying risks a duplicate.
    An agent keeps an admitted overture until a time its receipt states = decision:
      nid: 8fwdvgmg
      why: >-
        An agent MUST remember each overture it has admitted (its SAID in the requester's scope, its admission time and its status) for as long as it is pending, and its admitted receipt states a time until which the agent will also keep its outcome. Before that time the agent MUST NOT answer unknown about it; after it, unknown is allowed. A resend of an admitted overture (5c5fzw97 allows one before the receipt arrives) is answered with a receipt that repeats the original admission time, so notice (R13) has one time. Several agent AIDs at one endpoint share this memory, as 43lr8z7v already requires. R9's prerequisites, R13's notice and R15's fixed outcome all depend on that memory, which nothing required before (keri-review-panel findings SPC-F4 and SEC-F5). The agent chooses the time, because only it knows its storage policy; advice says to make it comfortably longer than a requester plausibly needs to poll. Lasting proof of what was done comes from the results, not from the agent's memory (7sx6spf2). We rejected letting an agent forget an outcome at any time once it is fixed, because a requester could not tell a five-minute-old outcome from a forgotten one and might retry into a duplicate, and rejected requiring outcomes to be kept forever, because that makes Overture an archive. The time is keptUntil, an absolute time on each admitted receipt item and absent on seen items; it is named as a statement of the agent's commitment rather than an instruction, unlike waitUntil, which is the requester's request. Tradeoff accepted: a requester that polls after the stated time may never learn the outcome from the agent.
    Over HTTP an agent answers in the response to the request that asked = decision:
      nid: 0y83jfj7
      why: >-
        In the HTTP binding, the agent's answer to an Overture message POSTed to its endpoint (2mgg0fn7) travels in that POST's response body as a CESR stream: a receipt or an inline status report for a batch, a status report for a poll or an abort. A 204 response means the agent has nothing to send, including when it refuses silently (R12). waitUntil is long polling as described in RFC 6202: the agent holds the response until it has an answer or the time passes, only for a message that has passed the R25 checks, within limits it sets (a maximum hold, a cap per requester AID), and it may always decline. keripy's HttpEnd queues every exn and answers 204, so it has no reply channel; the reference package (630f824j) extends it so that /overture/ routes are answered inline at the same URL, until keripy offers that (keri-review-panel finding GOV-F2). We rejected a mailbox at the agent, because it makes the agent store messages for strangers until they collect them, and server-sent events, because each answer is one signed status report that verifies alone and long polling carries over to bindings without event streams. Tradeoff accepted: an agent that holds connections spends sockets on them, bounded only by its own limits.
    A requester that wants pushes advertises its own endpoint with KERI replies = decision:
      nid: 4yzm2pn6
      why: >-
        An agent delivers on its own initiative only to an endpoint the requester advertises, and the requester advertises one by attaching KERI's own endpoint replies to its Overture message: /end/role/add authorizing the AID that receives for it (in the mailbox role, say) and /loc/scheme giving that AID's URL, the same mechanism agents use (0svwkvee). These replies are signed and governed by BADA, so they verify themselves and need no commitment in the body (8erq0hp5). The endpoint is usually a mailbox the requester chose, or the requester's own agent; the agent POSTs there and keeps nothing for the requester. A requester that attaches none gets no pushes and polls, which is also the private default. When a requester works through its own agent, that agent is the requester toward the other agent (R20, 4uvlnnx4) and advertises its own endpoint. We rejected having the agent resolve an OOBI for the requester, because it sends the agent looking for a stranger, and rejected letting an AID other than the requester poll, because it widens who sees an overture's status. Tradeoff accepted: a requester that signs its own overtures but wants another AID to poll for it must route through that AID as a hop.
    Attachments are framed as CESR v2 groups, KELs outside the message = decision:
      nid: 0xc2dqvw
      why: >-
        An Overture message is sent as a CESR v2 message + attachments group (-B, or --B when large), which encloses the message body (a JSON body inside a non-native body group, -H) followed by its attachments. Every attached message that the body commits to, whether a passed-on message (4uvlnnx4), an ACDC in an evidence chain or an attached result, is a nested message + attachments group (-B) among those attachments, which keripy's parser delivers to the handler as a nest (630f824j). An earlier text of this decision gave the codes as -U and -V, which are those groups' codes in keripy's version 1 table; in CESR v2, -U and -V are seal groups, and the CESR v2 universal table gives -B for message + attachments and -C for attachments only. KELs and KERI endpoint replies (4yzm2pn6) are not nested: they precede the Overture message in the same stream as top-level messages, so the receiver's ordinary KERI processing learns the key state before it verifies the Overture message. TEL events for attached credentials travel the same way. This answers keri-review-panel finding CSR-F4, which showed that a nested KEL is inert in keripy, since nests never reach Kevery, and that R10's "one CESR mechanism" named no count code. We rejected nesting KELs alongside the other attachments, because no KERI stack would process them. Tradeoff accepted: a message and the KELs it depends on are separate top-level messages in one stream, so a binding that carries one message per transmission must carry the KELs first.
    Passing an overture on is a disclosure that honors its evidence's terms = decision:
      nid: 4y5jkshj
      why: >-
        A hop that passes an overture on (4uvlnnx4) discloses the original message's attached evidence to the next agent, so it is a Discloser in ACDC's sense and MUST apply any terms of use the evidence's original disclosure carries, such as chain-link confidentiality; a hop that cannot pass the evidence on under its terms MUST NOT pass the overture on. A requester that does not want its evidence re-disclosed should not attach it to an overture it expects to be passed on (7gcqnese). This answers keri-review-panel finding PRV-F2. We rejected stripping evidence when passing on, because the original must arrive unaltered for its signature to verify, and rejected an agree step before evidence is disclosed, because IPEX already provides one for anyone who needs it. Tradeoff accepted: some overtures cannot be passed on at all, because their evidence forbids it.
    Unlinkable results do not use delegation as representation evidence = decision:
      nid: 7l9nr4dn
      why: >-
        A delegated AID names its delegator in its inception event's di field, which every party that resolves the AID can read. So an action whose result exists to keep the principal unlinkable, such as creating a relationship AID, SHOULD NOT use KERI delegation from the principal as the result's representation evidence (R6), and its catalog entry SHOULD specify evidence that is disclosed only to the requester, such as a GCD credential blinded with u, as its default. This answers keri-review-panel finding PRV-F3. We rejected forbidding delegation outright, because a principal may want the link public, and that is its choice. Tradeoff accepted: the default for such actions needs a GCD chain, which costs more to build and verify than a delegation.
    Minor versions add only what older readers may ignore; majors bind overtures = decision:
      nid: 0n0cy9mx
      why: >-
        version is written as two decimal integers, major.minor. Within a major version, a minor version may add optional fields, which an older reader ignores, except inside preconditions, where an unknown key makes the agent refuse the overture before admission (R5, 83e2qr0z); a new precondition therefore needs no major version, and an older agent refuses it, as it should. A new status value, a new receipt meaning or a changed meaning of any existing field needs a new major version. The JSON Schemas therefore allow additional properties everywhere except where a closed set is the point (statuses, meanings, signer values). An overture is bound to the major version of the message that first carried it: a resend MUST use the same major version, and an agent refuses a batch carrying a resend of an admitted overture under a different major version as malformed (7f9ydc0f). A message attached inside another is judged by its own version, and an agent that does not support it refuses the message that carries it. Because refusal may be silent (R12), a binding MAY report an unsupported major version at the transport layer without signing anything (the HTTP binding uses a 4xx error), which tells a requester that the version, not policy, was the problem. This answers keri-review-panel findings SPC-F6 and GOV-F5. We rejected putting version inside each overture, so that its SAID covers it, because the message's version already governs how every field in it is read and two places could disagree. Tradeoff accepted: an admitted overture cannot be carried forward into a new major version; the requester sends a new overture.
    Overture asks for issuance; IPEX carries the credential = decision:
      nid: 535qked4
      why: >-
        An IPEX apply asks a party to disclose a credential, and assumes the parties are already connected; it carries no admission, receipt, status, preconditions, abort, or proof of the requester's authority. Overture provides those for a stranger, and then launches the IPEX exchange: the agent answers an admitted issuance overture by starting IPEX at offer or grant, whose first message sets p to the exn SAID of the message that carried the overture, so the exchange points back at what started it. A requester already connected to an issuer may use IPEX apply directly; Overture does not replace it. The issuance catalog entry states which IPEX step its result is. This answers keri-review-panel finding SKP-F1. We rejected mapping the overture onto an apply, because apply comes from the disclosee in IPEX's own flow and has no slot for Overture's admission and status, and rejected removing issuance from the catalog, because asking a stranger for a credential is one of Overture's main uses. Tradeoff accepted: two ways to ask for a credential exist, one for strangers and one for connected parties.
    Audience stays, and its disclosure to the principal is stated = decision:
      nid: 4gqmgjxf
      why: >-
        The audience precondition (35d29vn5) stays in version 1. Understanding it means recognizing it: an agent that cannot produce a result bound to the audience and nonce SHOULD decline the overture (R5), and until some catalog entry defines a result that carries the binding, every agent will. The spec states plainly that audience tells the principal, everyone behind the counter and every hop which relying party the requester means to present the result to, and that the binding may keep that relying party's AID in the result; a requester that does not want that known should not use it. This answers keri-review-panel findings SKP-F2 and PRV-F4. We rejected deferring audience to a later version, because an extension added later would also be refused by older agents, so removing it saves nothing while losing the shape Daniel approved. Tradeoff accepted: audience has no conformance test that produces a bound result until a catalog entry defines one.
    Seen is the requester's acknowledgement of a status report = decision:
      nid: 6fcnv3q0
      why: >-
        An agent never signs a seen receipt for a message that carries overtures, so that notice runs only from an admitted receipt (R13) and an agent never signs about an overture it may still refuse (R12). seen has one use in version 1: a requester signs it over the exn SAID of a status report that gives outcomes, to tell the agent it has them; after that, the agent MAY forget those outcomes before their keptUntil time (8fwdvgmg). This answers keri-review-panel finding SKP-F3, which found no consumer for seen and showed that an agent-signed seen on a batch would look like notice. We rejected removing seen, because a requester's acknowledgement lets an agent free memory early, and rejected letting an agent sign seen on arrival, because it would sign about an overture before deciding whether to refuse it. Tradeoff accepted: seen is optional, so an agent cannot count on it and must still keep outcomes until keptUntil when it does not arrive.
    The agent role makes an AID an Overture answerer, and principals should know it = decision:
      nid: 8drpdx72
      why: >-
        Overture uses KERI's existing agent endpoint role (0svwkvee), which keripy defines although the KERI specification's role list does not yet include it. Some hosts, such as KERIA, already authorize an operator-keyed AID in that role for plumbing; if such a host also installs Overture's handlers, that AID becomes an Overture answerer for the principal, and its admitted receipts give notice. The spec says so in its security considerations, and says that an agent's statements carry only the weight it can prove (R20), so a requester relying on notice checks that weight. This answers keri-review-panel finding GOV-F3. We rejected a new role value, because keripy's role set is fixed in code, so a new role needs an upstream change before anyone can deploy, and because the agent role means what Overture needs: an AID that acts for the controller. Tradeoff accepted: a principal using such a host must decide deliberately whether to let it answer overtures.
    The reference package is Python on keripy's main branch, pinned by commit = decision:
      nid: 3wj8v5dl
      why: >-
        The reference package (630f824j) and the stand-in agent are Python, built on keripy, and depend on WebOfTrust/keripy's main branch pinned to one commit (0f1966f8 at first), managed with uv and tested with pytest. keripy is the only stack Overture's design has been checked against for v2 exn (ri, x), v2 nesting, KRAM and the Exchanger hooks the package must extend. We rejected the keri releases on PyPI, because the newest (1.3.6) predates v2 exn and cannot build an Overture message. We rejected TypeScript on signify-ts for the first package, because nothing in this design has been checked against it, and a requester library there follows once the message format has held up. Tradeoff accepted: adopters install keripy from a git commit until a v2 release reaches PyPI, and inherit keripy's Python 3.14 floor.
    Message schemas are plain JSON Schemas, one per route and major version = decision:
      nid: 2uvl4uuz
      why: >-
        Each route's a block has a JSON Schema in the 2020-12 dialect, at schemas/<major>/<route>.json, with an https $id where the file is published, and one shared schema for an overture object. These are not SAIDified, because nothing signs a reference to them: a message's version field already says which rules read it (3vpll4pd), and the schema for a major version stays valid for each of its minors, since minors only add optional fields and the schemas allow additional properties except in closed sets (0n0cy9mx). Catalog entries are different: an overture's signature commits to its entry by SAID, so entries are SAIDified (0nztkj2q). The schemas check shape only. A precondition key the agent does not understand passes the schema and is refused at step 11 of the check order, because that refusal is about understanding, not shape. We rejected SAIDifying the message schemas, because a SAID nobody references gives no protection and makes every editorial fix a new identifier. Tradeoff accepted: a message schema can change under its URL, so conformance is to the schema at a tagged release, not to whatever the URL serves today.
    A new AID states it is a result by sealing the overture's SAID at inception = decision:
      nid: 564x4rgx
      why: >-
        Where a catalog entry requires a result that can sign to state that it is a result of the overture (4662pten), as the relationship-AID entry does, the new AID makes that statement with a digest seal of the overture's SAID in the a field of its inception event. The statement is then signed by the new AID's own keys, verifiable from its KEL alone with ordinary KERI tools, and anchored rather than ephemeral, which a claim that must outlive rotation needs (7sx6spf2). The seal reveals nothing about the overture to someone who sees the KEL, because the overture's SAID is blinded by its u field. We rejected a separate exn signed by the new AID, because it would be ephemeral evidence, and rejected an interaction event after inception, because between the two events the AID would exist without the statement. Tradeoff accepted: the AID must be created with the overture in hand, so an AID made earlier cannot be offered as the result.
    Test vectors are generated from fixed seeds and checked by the test suite = decision:
      nid: 8kzvymmy
      why: >-
        Test vectors are CESR streams with their expected verdicts, stored as files in vectors/ and generated by a script in the repository from fixed salts and fixed timestamps, so the same inputs always give the same bytes. The test suite regenerates them and fails if any differs from the committed file, so a vector can never drift from the code that claims to produce it. A vector's file says, for each message, which checks it must pass or which step of the check order must refuse it. We rejected hand-written vectors, because signatures and SAIDs cannot be hand-written correctly, and rejected vectors made once and frozen, because nothing would notice when the code moved away from them. Tradeoff accepted: a change to keripy's serialization changes the vectors, and the change shows up as a diff to review rather than silently.
    The stand-in agent scripts its outcomes and keeps its memory in SQLite = decision:
      nid: 3yr7nvs7
      why: >-
        The stand-in agent, for client developers to build against before real agents exist (a deliverable in the requirements), runs the full check order and the full message set, but decides behind the counter by a script: per catalog entry, whether to admit, how long to stay pending, and which outcome to report with which canned results. It keeps the memory the spec requires (8fwdvgmg) in SQLite, so a restart does not make it answer unknown before keptUntil. We rejected an in-memory store, because it would break the rule the stand-in exists to teach the moment it restarted, and rejected LMDB through keripy's databases, because the agent's memory is Overture state rather than KERI state, and SQLite is easier for a developer to inspect. Tradeoff accepted: the stand-in's results are canned and do not verify as real credentials or AIDs unless the script says to make real ones.
    The reference package follows the error-code grammar without the errors package = decision:
      nid: 0s3s9prx
      why: >-
        The reference package names each reason it refuses a message or an overture with a code in the grammar of the error-code standard, such as e.input.format.f for a malformed batch, declared as module-scope literals, and logs it. The code never travels in a signed message, because refusal may be silent (R12) and reasons are omitted by default (36j5xvln). The one unsigned answer that can carry a problem, the HTTP 400 for an unsupported major version, uses RFC 9457 with type about:blank. We rejected depending on bakobo/errors, because the package is meant to move to keripy and the KERI community (630f824j), and a protocol's reference code should not bring one company's catalog and its URLs with it. Tradeoff accepted: the package matches codes with its own few lines of prefix matching rather than the shared matcher.
    The first catalog entries are drafts that place each action on the GCD grid = decision:
      nid: 4zmcmynl
      why: >-
        The initial catalog entries, enough to run the test cases, are in catalog/, one SAIDified file per entry. Each places its action on the GCD 4.0 act grid: issuing a credential is create record, with the issuee as target; revoking one is modify record, because revocation changes the credential's status in its registry and does not unmake it, with the credential as target; asking to be introduced is create relationship, with the party to be introduced to as target; accepting an introduction, the entry a hop uses to pass that request on (4uvlnnx4), is create relationship, with the party asking to be introduced as target; asking to be challenged is observe record, with no target, because the act lands on the requester who asked; creating a relationship AID is create relationship, with no target beyond the requester; and starting another protocol is create relationship, with no target. These placements are first judgments, recorded so that a policy author can disagree with something specific. We rejected leaving the overture annotation out of the first entries, because a policy engine cannot check an overture against a GCD credential without it. Tradeoff accepted: a placement that turns out wrong means a new entry with a new SAID, and overtures already signed keep the old one.
    Attached evidence is verified as ordinary ACDCs at step 8, and a link that fails makes the batch malformed = decision:
      nid: 2s0z8frg
      why: >-
        At step 8 of the check order, for each SAID in an overture's evidence, at every depth of nesting, the message carrying that overture MUST carry a nested ACDC with that SAID, and that ACDC and every ACDC its edges reach MUST verify by ACDC's own rules against the key state and TEL state the agent holds when it receives the batch: the ACDC's SAID, its schema, its issuer being the controller of the registry its status names, its issuance in that registry and not revoked, and each edge with its operator. A credential the agent cannot check because it lacks the issuer's KEL, the registry's TEL or a schema is refused with a retryable reason, as missing key state is (35wllumn); the requester sends the TEL events before the batch as it sends KELs (8erq0hp5). Any failure makes the batch malformed and the agent refuses it whole (7f9ydc0f), because the requester's signature commits to evidence that does not hold. An attached ACDC that no evidence SAID reaches is not checked, because it carries no claim (8erq0hp5). Whether verified evidence is enough authority for the action stays behind the counter. We rejected leaving evidence checks to policy, because two agents could then disagree on whether one batch is well formed, and an agent could admit, and so give notice of, an overture resting on a revoked credential. We rejected refusing only the overture whose evidence fails, because a bad commitment shows the batch was built wrongly, as a bad SAID does. Tradeoff accepted: an agent pays to verify a stranger's chain before deciding whether to admit, bounded by the size limit at step 1, and must hold the schema of every credential in a chain it is shown.
    The reference agent pushes each outcome once to the requester's mailbox or agent, retrying until it is delivered or forgotten = decision:
      nid: 97dehty6
      why: >-
        The reference package delivers on the agent's initiative (4yzm2pn6) to each URL that a /loc/scheme reply gives for an AID the requester authorized in the mailbox or agent role by /end/role/add, these being the roles of an AID that receives for another. It pushes when an overture reaches an outcome, sending one signed status report covering the requester's outcomes not yet delivered, the same message a poll would return, and counts them delivered when one URL answers with a 2xx status. Undelivered outcomes are tried again on the agent's next pass until delivery succeeds or the outcome is forgotten at keptUntil (8fwdvgmg). A push never replaces polling: a requester may still poll for an outcome already pushed, and its seen receipt works the same either way (6fcnv3q0). Pending statuses are not pushed, because a requester learns nothing from them that its receipt's nextPoll did not say. Because the URL comes from a stranger's signed replies, a push is a request the requester aims, so the agent connects only to public addresses unless its operator allows others, checks the address it actually connected to rather than the one a name first resolved to, and follows no redirect; otherwise any requester could make the agent POST into the agent's own network. We rejected pushing to every role, because a witness or watcher the requester authorized does not receive messages for it, and rejected one attempt only, because an outcome a requester never hears of costs it a poll it may not know to make. Tradeoff accepted: an outcome may be delivered twice when a push succeeds but its answer is lost, so a requester must expect to see the same outcome in more than one status report.
facet = term:
  id: 7v6n52dz
  definition: >-
    One of the many aspects in which a single entity acts, kept isolated from its other facets, so that only the entity decides who may learn that two facets are the same entity. Each facet has its own AID, and a principal is always one facet. See Identity Facets, https://dhh1128.github.io/papers/if.html.
overture = term:
  id: 2q2c2dfl
  definition: >-
    A signed request from a requester asking a principal to take exactly one action. Its own SAID, which covers a nonce the requester makes fresh for it, identifies it within the requester's scope, so two sends carrying the same SAID are the same overture (7erf49uk, 4g7vjv8w).
batch = term:
  id: 54m97zvg
  definition: >-
    A single message that carries one or more overtures. Overtures in a batch may depend on one another through dependency links, but each keeps its own lifecycle, and a batch is never all-or-nothing (8zysn4nv).
outcome = term:
  id: 8hf8lm3p
  definition: >-
    What became of an overture: the principal acted on it or declined it, or it ended by an event such as expiry or an honored abort. The agent reports the outcome, with any evidence, in its last message about that overture (0d2s9emg).
endpoint = term:
  id: 75jkh39e
  definition: >-
    The location a requester sends to: the URL a principal's agent publishes by a /loc/scheme reply, which is the agent's general KERI ingress. Any agent AID the principal has authorized there by /end/role/add may answer. This differs from KERI's sense, in which an endpoint is identified by one eid (2mgg0fn7).
requester = term:
  id: 5dnehv9p
  definition: >-
    The party that sends an overture, identified by its AID (R23). Who controls that AID may remain unknown.
agent = term:
  id: 4m52t924
  definition: >-
    A party, identified by its AID, that receives overtures for exactly one principal and is the only party that answers the requester in Overture. The principal authorizes it as an endpoint, and may authorize several agents (0svwkvee). An agent refuses, before admission, an overture that references a principal it does not act for (R3).
principal = term:
  id: 2k8fxl77
  definition: >-
    The AID an agent acts for: one facet of whatever entity stands behind it. An overture references it (R3), but it never takes part in the protocol.
behind the counter = term:
  id: 66u9sk4r
  definition: >-
    Everything between the agent and the principal, such as a policy engine, escalation to the principal, or nested layers of agents. The requester deals only with whoever answers at the endpoint, as a customer deals with whoever stands at a service counter, and the protocol never discloses what is behind it.
